-- title:
--   Finiteness of a two-generated Hopf algebra with torsion norm-one points
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero, let $c \in K$ be nonzero and not a square, let $n$ be a prime, and let $\delta$ in the algebraic closure $\overline{K}$ satisfy $\delta^2 = c$ (the image of $c$ under the structure map $K \to \overline{K}$). Let $A$ be a commutative ring equipped with a $K$-Hopf algebra structure, and let $u', v' \in A$ be elements such that the $K$-subalgebra generated by $\{u', v'\}$ is all of $A$ and such that $u'^2 - c\,v'^2 = 1$ in $A$. Assume further: (i) for every $K$-algebra homomorphism $f \colon A \to \overline{K}$ one has $(f(u') + f(v')\,\delta)^n = 1$; and (ii) for every pair $(w, z)$ of elements of $\overline{K}$ with $w^2 - c\,z^2 = 1$ and $(w + z\delta)^n = 1$ there is exactly one $K$-algebra homomorphism $f \colon A \to \overline{K}$ with $f(u') = w$ and $f(v') = z$. Then $A$ is a finite $K$-module, i.e. finitely generated as a $K$-vector space.
--
--   This is the finiteness half of the analysis of the $n$-torsion of the norm-one torus of $K(\sqrt{c})$ presented as a Hopf algebra: the hypotheses say that the $\overline{K}$-points of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ are exactly the norm-one pairs $(w,z)$ whose associated element $w + z\delta$ is an $n$-th root of unity, and the conclusion is that $A$ is a finite-dimensional $K$-algebra. It is used by [`HopfAlgebra.exists_finite_nTorsion_quotient_of_normOneTorus_generators`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.exists_finite_nTorsion_quotient_of_normOneTorus_generators), which combines it with the construction of the corresponding Hopf quotient.
